 ((1
  ("Under consideration for publication in J. Functional Programming" 0.1963 0.6135 0.1404 0.1488 0.1488 0.0084 0.0051 0.2746 0.0388)
  ("1" 0.7904 0.7988 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("FUNCTIONAL PEARL" 0.3264 0.6686 0.1955 0.2136 0.2136 0.0182 0.0146 0.1640 0.2190)
  ("Applicative programming with effects" 0.2708 0.7242 0.2281 0.2463 0.2463 0.0182 0.0101 0.3128 0.1360)
  ("CONOR MCBRIDE" 0.4223 0.5727 0.2685 0.2790 0.2790 0.0105 0.0056 0.2353 0.0630)
  ("University of Nottingham" 0.4038 0.5913 0.2839 0.2944 0.2944 0.0105 0.0056 0.2891 0.0750)
  ("ROSS PATERSON" 0.4271 0.5679 0.3077 0.3182 0.3182 0.0105 0.0056 0.1910 0.0435)
  ("City University, London" 0.4095 0.5856 0.3231 0.3336 0.3336 0.0105 0.0056 0.3013 0.0316)
  ("Abstract" 0.4607 0.5343 0.3691 0.3796 0.3796 0.0105 nil 0.2463 0.0736)
  ("In this paper, we introduce Applicative functors—an abstract characterisation of an ap-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3926 0.4021 0.4021 0.0095 0.0063 0.2461 0.0142)
  ("plicative style of effectful programming, weaker than Monads and hence more widespread." 0.1963 0.7988 0.4056 0.4151 0.4151 0.0095 0.0046 0.3015 0.0568)
  ("Indeed, it is the ubiquity of this programming pattern that drew us to the abstraction." 0.1963 0.7988 0.4187 0.4281 0.4281 0.0095 0.0061 0.2395 0.0495)
  ("We retrace our steps in this paper, introducing the applicative pattern by diverse exam-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4317 0.4411 0.4411 0.0095 0.0055 0.2489 0.0215)
  ("ples, then abstracting it to define the Applicative type class and introducing a bracket" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4447 0.4541 0.4541 0.0095 0.0069 0.2079 0.0302)
  ("notation which interprets the normal application syntax in the idiom of an Applicative" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4577 0.4672 0.4672 0.0095 0.0068 0.2564 0.0568)
  ("functor. Further, we develop the properties of applicative functors and the generic opera-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4707 0.4802 0.4802 0.0095 0.0050 0.2256 0.0529)
  ("tions they support. We close by identifying the categorical structure of applicative functors" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4837 0.4932 0.4932 0.0095 0.0039 0.2850 0.0328)
  ("and examining their relationship both with Monads and with Arrows." 0.1963 0.6648 0.4968 0.5062 0.5062 0.0095 0.0052 0.2937 0.0249)
  ("1 Introduction" 0.4348 0.5602 0.5592 0.5697 0.5697 0.0105 0.0096 0.1761 0.0096)
  ("This is the story of a pattern that popped up time and again in our daily work," 0.1963 0.7988 0.5824 0.5929 0.5929 0.0105 0.0067 0.2278 0.0326)
  ("programming in Haskell (Peyton Jones, 2003), until the temptation to abstract it" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5977 0.6082 0.6082 0.0105 0.0061 0.2713 0.0977)
  ("became irresistable. Let us illustrate with some examples." 0.1963 0.6193 0.6131 0.6236 0.6236 0.0105 0.0056 0.3081 0.0544)
  ("Sequencing commands One often wants to execute a sequence of commands and" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6446 0.6551 0.6551 0.0105 0.0070 0.2274 0.0800)
  ("collect the sequence of their responses, and indeed there is such a function in the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6600 0.6705 0.6705 0.0105 0.0062 0.2028 0.0465)
  ("Haskell Prelude (here specialised to IO):" 0.1963 0.4900 0.6753 0.6858 0.6858 0.0105 0.0056 0.2804 0.0526)
  ("sequence :: [IO a ] → IO [a ]" 0.2381 0.4301 0.6977 0.7088 0.7088 0.0105 0.0046 0.4669 0.0621)
  ("sequence [ ]" 0.2381 0.3321 0.7137 0.7242 0.7242 0.0105 0.0198 0.5016 0.0621)
  ("= return [ ]" 0.3592 0.4358 0.7137 0.7242 0.7242 0.0105 0.0046 0.4077 0.0130)
  ("sequence (c : cs) = do" 0.2381 0.3954 0.7291 0.7396 0.7396 0.0105 0.0046 0.2975 0.0621)
  ("x ←c" 0.2548 0.3046 0.7438 0.7550 0.7550 0.0112 0.0129 0.4011 0.0078)
  ("xs ← sequence cs" 0.2548 0.3782 0.7592 0.7704 0.7704 0.0105 0.0046 0.3462 0.0146)
  ("return (x : xs)" 0.2548 0.3526 0.7752 0.7857 0.7857 0.0105 0.0046 0.2354 0.0422)
  ("In the (c : cs) case, we collect the values of some effectful computations, which we" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7980 0.8085 0.8085 0.0105 0.0057 0.2832 0.0153)
  ("then use as the arguments to a pure function (:). We could avoid the need for names" 0.1963 0.7988 0.8134 0.8239 0.8239 0.0105 0.0045 0.3141 0.0326)
  ("to wire these values through to their point of usage if we had a kind of ‘effectful" 0.1963 0.7988 0.8287 0.8393 0.8393 0.0105 0.0065 0.2203 0.0149)
  ("application’. Fortunately, exactly such a thing lives in the standard Monad library:" 0.1963 0.7988 0.8441 0.8546 0.8546 0.0105 0.0053 0.2624 0.0902))
  (2
  ("2" 0.1963 0.2046 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("Conor McBride and Ross Paterson" 0.3687 0.6263 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 0.0060 0.2641 0.0456)
  ("ap :: Monad m ⇒ m (a → b) → m a → m b" 0.2381 0.5516 0.1620 0.1731 0.1731 0.0105 0.0046 0.4922 0.0167)
  ("ap mf mx = do" 0.2381 0.3508 0.1780 0.1885 0.1885 0.0105 0.0067 0.3668 0.0167)
  ("f ← mf" 0.2548 0.3095 0.1927 0.2039 0.2039 0.0112 0.0093 0.4775 0.0051)
  ("x ← mx" 0.2548 0.3121 0.2081 0.2193 0.2193 0.0112 0.0067 0.4600 0.0078)
  ("return (f x )" 0.2548 0.3378 0.2242 0.2347 0.2347 0.0105 0.0046 0.2908 0.0422)
  ("Using this function we could rewrite sequence as:" 0.1963 0.5549 0.2458 0.2563 0.2563 0.0105 0.0056 0.2577 0.0415)
  ("sequence :: [IO a ] → IO [a ]" 0.2381 0.4301 0.2671 0.2782 0.2782 0.0105 0.0046 0.4669 0.0621)
  ("sequence [ ]" 0.2381 0.3321 0.2831 0.2936 0.2936 0.0105 0.0198 0.5016 0.0621)
  ("= return [ ]" 0.3592 0.4358 0.2831 0.2936 0.2936 0.0105 0.0046 0.4077 0.0130)
  ("sequence (c : cs) = return (:) ‘ap‘ c ‘ap‘ sequence cs" 0.2381 0.5982 0.2985 0.3090 0.3090 0.0105 0.0046 0.2807 0.0621)
  ("where the return operation, which every Monad must provide, lifts pure values to" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3202 0.3307 0.3307 0.0105 0.0064 0.2541 0.0428)
  ("the effectful world, whilst ap provides ‘application’ within it." 0.1963 0.6405 0.3355 0.3461 0.3461 0.0105 0.0056 0.2889 0.0232)
  ("Except for the noise of the returns and aps, this definition is in a fairly standard" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3509 0.3614 0.3614 0.0105 0.0055 0.2414 0.0509)
  ("applicative style, even though effects are present." 0.1963 0.5555 0.3663 0.3768 0.3768 0.0105 0.0056 0.2092 0.0790)
  ("Transposing ‘matrices’ Suppose we represent matrices (somewhat approximately)" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3970 0.4075 0.4075 0.0105 0.0063 0.2641 0.0870)
  ("by lists of lists. A common operation on matrices is transposition^{1} ." 0.1963 0.6872 0.4106 0.4229 0.4229 0.0105 0.0056 0.3236 0.0177)
  ("transpose :: [[a ]] → [[a ]]" 0.2381 0.4151 0.4336 0.4448 0.4448 0.0105 0.0037 0.4209 0.0662)
  ("transpose" 0.2381 0.3043 0.4497 0.4602 0.4602 0.0105 nil 0.1699 0.0662)
  ("[]" 0.3314 0.3435 0.4497 0.4602 0.4602 0.0105 nil nil 0.0121)
  ("= repeat [ ]" 0.3775 0.4557 0.4497 0.4602 0.4602 0.0105 0.0046 0.3983 0.0130)
  ("transpose (xs : xss) = zipWith (:) xs (transpose xss)" 0.2381 0.6039 0.4651 0.4756 0.4756 0.0105 0.0047 0.3054 0.0662)
  ("Now, the binary zipWith is one of a family of operations that ‘vectorise’ pure func-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4867 0.4972 0.4972 0.0105 0.0053 0.3055 0.0372)
  ("tions. As Daniel Fridlender and Mia Indrika (2000) point out, the entire family can" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5021 0.5126 0.5126 0.0105 0.0050 0.3115 0.0401)
  ("be generated from repeat, which generates an infinite stream from its argument," 0.1963 0.7988 0.5175 0.5280 0.5280 0.0105 0.0071 0.2647 0.0172)
  ("and zapp, a kind of ‘zippy’ application." 0.1963 0.4818 0.5329 0.5434 0.5434 0.0105 0.0056 0.2526 0.0270)
  ("zapp :: [a → b ] → [a ] → [b ]" 0.4967 0.6976 0.5465 0.5576 0.5576 0.0105 0.0046 0.5693 0.0326)
  ("repeat :: a → [a ]" 0.2548 0.3732 0.5503 0.5615 0.5615 0.0105 0.0037 0.4664 0.0438)
  ("zapp (f : fs) (x : xs) = f x : zapp fs xs" 0.4967 0.7640 0.5625 0.5730 0.5730 0.0105 0.0047 0.2897 0.0326)
  ("repeat x = x : repeat x" 0.2548 0.4135 0.5664 0.5769 0.5769 0.0105 0.0047 0.2926 0.0438)
  ("= []" 0.6413 0.6711 0.5779 0.5884 0.5884 0.0105 0.0046 0.4164 0.0130)
  ("zapp" 0.4967 0.5294 0.5779 0.5884 0.5884 0.0105 nil 0.0702 0.0326)
  ("The general scheme is as follows:" 0.2130 0.4538 0.5996 0.6101 0.6101 0.0105 0.0056 0.3001 0.0288)
  ("zipWith_{n} :: (a_{1} → · · · → a_{n} → b) → [a_{1} ] → · · · → [a_{n} ] → [b]" 0.2657 0.7004 0.6220 0.6354 0.6332 0.0112 0.0046 0.6019 0.0627)
  ("zipWith_{n} f xs_{1} . . . xs_{n} = repeat f ‘zapp‘ xs_{1} ‘zapp‘ . . . ‘zapp‘ xs_{n}" 0.2657 0.7285 0.6380 0.6507 0.6485 0.0105 0.0037 0.4002 0.0627)
  ("In particular, transposition becomes" 0.2130 0.4779 0.6597 0.6702 0.6702 0.0105 0.0056 0.2641 0.0153)
  ("transpose :: [[a ]] → [[a ]]" 0.2381 0.4151 0.6809 0.6921 0.6921 0.0105 0.0037 0.4209 0.0662)
  ("transpose" 0.2381 0.3043 0.6970 0.7075 0.7075 0.0105 nil 0.1699 0.0662)
  ("[]" 0.3314 0.3435 0.6970 0.7075 0.7075 0.0105 nil nil 0.0121)
  ("= repeat [ ]" 0.3775 0.4557 0.6970 0.7075 0.7075 0.0105 0.0046 0.3988 0.0130)
  ("transpose (xs : xss) = repeat (:) ‘zapp‘ xs ‘zapp‘ transpose xss" 0.2381 0.6683 0.7124 0.7229 0.7229 0.0105 0.0046 0.2627 0.0662)
  ("Except for the noise of the repeats and zapps, this definition is in a fairly standard" 0.2130 0.7988 0.7340 0.7445 0.7445 0.0105 0.0044 0.2781 0.0509)
  ("applicative style, even though we are working with vectors." 0.1963 0.6294 0.7494 0.7599 0.7599 0.0105 0.0056 0.2444 0.0790)
  ("Evaluating expressions When implementing an evaluator for a language of expres-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7801 0.7906 0.7906 0.0105 0.0055 0.2831 0.0772)
  ("sions, it is customary to pass around an environment, giving values to the free" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7955 0.8060 0.8060 0.0105 0.0078 0.2259 0.0402)
  ("variables. Here is a very simple example" 0.1963 0.4896 0.8109 0.8214 0.8214 0.0105 0.0056 0.3157 0.0685)
  ("^{1} This function differs from the one in the standard library in its treatment of ragged lists" 0.1988 0.7611 0.8441 0.8542 0.8542 0.0084 0.0048 0.2695 0.0061))
