  ("​| Val Int" 0.3258 0.3860 0.1774 0.1885 0.1885 0.0105 0.0088 0.3537 0.0047)
  ("​| Add (Exp v ) (Exp v )" 0.3258 0.4841 0.1927 0.2039 0.2039 0.0105 0.0046 0.3573 0.0047)
  ("eval :: Exp v → Env v → Int" 0.2381 0.4347 0.2115 0.2227 0.2227 0.0105 0.0046 0.4522 0.0272)
  ("eval (Var x ) γ = fetch x γ" 0.2381 0.4385 0.2275 0.2380 0.2380 0.0105 0.0049 0.5052 0.0272)
  ("eval (Val i )" 0.2381 0.3172 0.2429 0.2534 0.2534 0.0105 0.0046 0.3892 0.0272)
  ("γ=i" 0.3442 0.3812 0.2429 0.2534 0.2534 0.0105 nil 0.1073 0.0370)
  ("eval (Add p q) γ = eval p γ + eval q γ" 0.2381 0.5066 0.2583 0.2688 0.2688 0.0105 0.0046 0.3307 0.0272)
  ("where Env v is some notion of environment and fetch x projects the value for the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.2804 0.2910 0.2910 0.0105 0.0062 0.2385 0.0428)
  ("variable x ." 0.1963 0.2735 0.2958 0.3063 0.3063 0.0105 0.0056 0.3128 0.0572)
  ("We can eliminate the clutter of the explicitly threaded environment with a little" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3113 0.3219 0.3219 0.0105 0.0054 0.3069 0.0232)
  ("help from some very old friends, designed for this purpose:" 0.1963 0.6261 0.3267 0.3372 0.3372 0.0105 0.0056 0.2762 0.0307)
  ("eval :: Exp v → Env v → Int" 0.2381 0.4347 0.3484 0.3596 0.3596 0.0105 0.0046 0.4522 0.0272)
  ("eval (Var x ) = fetch x" 0.2381 0.4089 0.3645 0.3750 0.3750 0.0105 0.0047 0.5422 0.0272)
  ("eval (Val i )" 0.2381 0.3172 0.3799 0.3904 0.3904 0.0105 0.0046 0.3892 0.0272)
  ("=Ki" 0.3442 0.3846 0.3799 0.3922 0.3904 0.0105 nil 0.0001 0.0405)
  ("eval (Add p q) = K (+) ‘S‘ eval p ‘S‘ eval q" 0.2381 0.5386 0.3953 0.4076 0.4058 0.0105 0.0046 0.3770 0.0272)
  ("where" 0.1963 0.2391 0.4174 0.4279 0.4279 0.0105 nil 0.2393 0.0428)
  ("K :: a → env → a" 0.2548 0.3817 0.4288 0.4418 0.4400 0.0112 0.0046 0.5365 0.0130)
  ("Kx γ=x" 0.2548 0.3266 0.4449 0.4572 0.4554 0.0105 0.0067 0.3704 0.0254)
  ("S :: (env → a → b) → (env → a) → (env → b)" 0.4363 0.7746 0.4288 0.4418 0.4400 0.0112 0.0046 0.4891 0.0093)
  ("S ef es γ = (ef γ) (es γ)" 0.4363 0.6152 0.4449 0.4572 0.4554 0.0105 0.0060 0.2697 0.0093)
  ("Except for the noise of the K and S combinators^{2} , this definition of eval is in a" 0.2130 0.7988 0.4663 0.4805 0.4786 0.0105 0.0061 0.2794 0.0509)
  ("fairly standard applicative style, even though we are abstracting an environment." 0.1963 0.7918 0.4835 0.4940 0.4940 0.0105 0.0056 0.2397 0.0382)
  ("2 The Applicative class" 0.4072 0.5878 0.5249 0.5354 0.5354 0.0105 0.0064 0.2966 0.0096)
  ("We have seen three examples of this ‘pure function applied to funny arguments’" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5481 0.5587 0.5587 0.0105 0.0070 0.2505 0.0232)
  ("pattern in apparently quite diverse fields—let us now abstract out what they have" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5635 0.5740 0.5740 0.0105 0.0055 0.2680 0.0540)
  ("in common. In each example, there is a type constructor f that embeds the usual" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5789 0.5894 0.5894 0.0105 0.0060 0.2632 0.0140)
  ("notion of value, but supports its own peculiar way of giving meaning to the usual" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5943 0.6048 0.6048 0.0105 0.0060 0.2407 0.0465)
  ("applicative language—its idiom. We correspondingly introduce the Applicative class:" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6097 0.6202 0.6202 0.0105 0.0041 0.3360 0.0790)
  ("infixl 4 ~" 0.2381 0.3040 0.6321 0.6444 0.6426 0.0105 0.0046 0.3495 0.0361)
  ("class Applicative f where" 0.2381 0.4184 0.6508 0.6613 0.6613 0.0105 0.0047 0.3106 0.0354)
  ("pure :: a → f a" 0.2548 0.3598 0.6655 0.6767 0.6767 0.0105 0.0046 0.4728 0.0304)
  ("(~) :: f (a → b) → f a → f b" 0.2548 0.4693 0.6809 0.6921 0.6921 0.0105 0.0059 0.4900 0.0260)
  ("This class generalises S and K from threading an environment to threading an effect" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7037 0.7161 0.7142 0.0105 0.0043 0.3073 0.0326)
  ("in general." 0.1963 0.2726 0.7191 0.7296 0.7296 0.0105 0.0056 0.2328 0.0140)
  ("We shall require the following laws for applicative functors:" 0.2130 0.6477 0.7346 0.7451 0.7451 0.0105 0.0056 0.2985 0.0232)
  ("identity" 0.1963 0.2535 0.7571 0.7676 0.7676 0.0105 nil 0.2924 0.0573)
  ("pure id ~ u = u" 0.3910 0.5116 0.7571 0.7695 0.7676 0.0105 0.0047 0.3929 0.0304)
  ("composition" 0.1963 0.2882 0.7725 0.7830 0.7830 0.0105 nil 0.3422 0.0920)
  ("pure (·) ~ u ~ v ~ w = u ~ (v ~ w )" 0.3227 0.5892 0.7725 0.7849 0.7830 0.0123 0.0047 0.4495 0.0304)
  ("homomorphism" 0.1963 0.3126 0.7879 0.7984 0.7984 0.0105 nil 0.3161 0.1164)
  ("pure f ~ pure x = pure (f x )" 0.3604 0.5738 0.7879 0.8003 0.7984 0.0105 0.0047 0.3494 0.0304)
  ("interchange" 0.1963 0.2850 0.8033 0.8138 0.8138 0.0105 nil 0.2037 0.0887)
  ("u ~ pure x = pure (λf → f x ) ~ u" 0.3939 0.6477 0.8026 0.8156 0.8138 0.0105 0.0047 0.4259 0.0090)
  ("^{2} also known as the return and ap of the environment Monad" 0.1988 0.5768 0.8441 0.8542 0.8542 0.0084 0.0047 0.2605 0.0061))
  (4
  ("4" 0.1963 0.2046 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("Conor McBride and Ross Paterson" 0.3687 0.6263 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 0.0060 0.2641 0.0456)
  ("The idea is that pure embeds pure computations into the pure fragment of an" 0.2038 0.7988 0.1672 0.1778 0.1778 0.0105 0.0076 0.2295 0.0288)
  ("effectful world—the resulting computations may thus be shunted around freely, as" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1826 0.1931 0.1931 0.0105 0.0057 0.2897 0.0577)
  ("long as the order of the genuinely effectful computations is preserved." 0.1963 0.7059 0.1980 0.2085 0.2085 0.0105 0.0056 0.2393 0.0307)
  ("You can easily check that applicative functors are indeed functors, with the fol-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.2135 0.2240 0.2240 0.0105 0.0058 0.2322 0.0288)
  ("lowing action on functions:" 0.1963 0.3935 0.2289 0.2394 0.2394 0.0105 0.0056 0.2597 0.0469)
  ("(<$>) :: Applicative f ⇒ (a → b) → f a → f b" 0.2381 0.5652 0.2506 0.2617 0.2617 0.0105 0.0046 0.4869 0.0400)
  ("f <$> u = pure f ~ u" 0.2381 0.3851 0.2666 0.2790 0.2771 0.0105 0.0047 0.4209 0.0051)
  ("Moreover, any expression built from the Applicative combinators can be transformed" 0.1963 0.7988 0.2887 0.2992 0.2992 0.0105 0.0041 0.3269 0.0726)
  ("to a canonical form in which a single pure function is ‘applied’ to the effectful parts" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3041 0.3146 0.3146 0.0105 0.0048 0.2919 0.0149)
  ("in depth-first order:" 0.1963 0.3406 0.3195 0.3300 0.3300 0.0105 0.0056 0.2321 0.0140)
  ("pure f ~ u_{1} ~ . . . ~ u_{n}" 0.4179 0.5763 0.3388 0.3512 0.3493 0.0123 0.0037 0.4834 0.0304)
  ("This canonical form captures the essence of Applicative programming: computa-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3583 0.3688 0.3688 0.0105 0.0058 0.2900 0.0326)
  ("tions have a fixed structure, given by the pure function, and a sequence of sub-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3737 0.3842 0.3842 0.0105 0.0072 0.1841 0.0354)
  ("computations, given by the effectful arguments. We therefore find it convenient, at" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3890 0.3996 0.3996 0.0105 0.0053 0.2933 0.1033)
  ("least within this paper, to write this form using a special bracket notation," 0.1963 0.7446 0.4044 0.4149 0.4149 0.0105 0.0056 0.2692 0.0336)
  ("J f u_{1} . . . u_{n} K" 0.4478 0.5473 0.4279 0.4498 0.4384 0.0149 0.0028 0.4097 0.0067)
  ("indicating a shift into the idiom of an Applicative functor, where a pure function is" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4514 0.4619 0.4619 0.0105 0.0056 0.2765 0.0725)
  ("applied to a sequence of effectful arguments using the appropriate ~. Our intention" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4668 0.4791 0.4773 0.0105 0.0048 0.2845 0.0530)
  ("is to give an indication that effects are present, whilst retaining readability of code." 0.1963 0.7988 0.4821 0.4927 0.4927 0.0105 0.0049 0.2568 0.0112)
  ("Given Haskell extended with multi-parameter type classes, enthusiasts for over-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.4976 0.5082 0.5082 0.0105 0.0059 0.2741 0.0429)
  ("loading may replace ‘J’ and ‘K’ by identifiers iI and Ii with the right behaviour^{3} ." 0.1963 0.7757 0.5112 0.5235 0.5235 0.0105 0.0056 0.3045 0.0530)
  ("The IO monad, and indeed any Monad, can be made Applicative by taking pure =" 0.2130 0.7988 0.5285 0.5390 0.5390 0.0105 0.0048 0.3180 0.0288)
  ("return and (~) = ap. We could alternatively use the variant of ap that performs" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5439 0.5544 0.5544 0.0105 0.0069 0.2589 0.0422)
  ("the computations in the opposite order, but we shall keep to the left-to-right order" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5593 0.5698 0.5698 0.0105 0.0053 0.2519 0.0232)
  ("in this paper. Sometimes we can implement the Applicative interface a little more" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5747 0.5852 0.5852 0.0105 0.0062 0.3131 0.0140)
  ("directly, as with (→) env :" 0.1963 0.3848 0.5901 0.6006 0.6006 0.0105 0.0056 0.3946 0.0586)
  ("instance Applicative ((→) env ) where" 0.2381 0.5108 0.6131 0.6236 0.6236 0.0105 0.0046 0.3695 0.0624)
  ("pure x = λγ → x" 0.2548 0.3846 0.6278 0.6390 0.6390 0.0105 0.0047 0.4437 0.0304)
  ("-- K" 0.4866 0.5164 0.6285 0.6408 0.6408 0.0105 0.0056 0.0012 0.0111)
  ("ef ~ ex = λγ → (ef γ) (ex γ) -- S" 0.2548 0.5127 0.6432 0.6562 0.6543 0.0105 0.0056 0.4043 0.0128)
  ("With these instances, sequence and eval become:" 0.1963 0.5508 0.6659 0.6765 0.6765 0.0105 0.0056 0.2951 0.0377)
  ("sequence :: [IO a ] → IO [a ]" 0.2381 0.4301 0.6876 0.6988 0.6988 0.0105 0.0046 0.4669 0.0621)
  ("sequence [ ]" 0.2381 0.3321 0.7037 0.7142 0.7142 0.0105 0.0198 0.5016 0.0621)
  ("^{=} J^{[ ]}K" 0.3592 0.4061 0.7037 0.7256 0.7256 0.0149 0.0046 0.5002 0.0130)
  ("sequence (c : cs) = J (:) c (sequence cs) K" 0.2381 0.5253 0.7191 0.7410 0.7296 0.0105 0.0046 0.2983 0.0621)
  ("eval :: Exp v → Env v → Int" 0.2381 0.4347 0.7371 0.7483 0.7483 0.0105 0.0046 0.4522 0.0272)
  ("eval (Var x ) = fetch x" 0.2381 0.4089 0.7532 0.7637 0.7637 0.0105 0.0047 0.5422 0.0272)
  ("eval (Val i )" 0.2381 0.3172 0.7686 0.7791 0.7791 0.0105 0.0046 0.3892 0.0272)
  ("^{=} J^{i} K" 0.3442 0.3859 0.7686 0.7905 0.7905 0.0149 0.0046 0.4939 0.0130)
  ("eval (Add p q) = J (+) (eval p) (eval q) K" 0.2381 0.5229 0.7840 0.8059 0.7945 0.0105 0.0046 0.3607 0.0272)
  ("If we want to do the same for our transpose example, we shall have to avoid the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.8061 0.8166 0.8166 0.0105 0.0067 0.2377 0.0112)
  ("^{3} Hint: Define an overloaded function applicative u v1" 0.1988 0.5321 0.8441 0.8542 0.8542 0.0084 0.0047 0.2729 0.0061)
  (". . . vn Ii = u ~ v1 ~ . . . ~ vn" 0.5402 0.7165 0.8458 0.8557 0.8542 0.0084 0.0032 0.5137 0.0040))
  (5
  ("Functional pearl" 0.4387 0.5563 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 0.0060 0.2161 0.0772)
  ("5" 0.7904 0.7988 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("library’s ‘list of successes’ (Wadler, 1985) monad and take instead an instance" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1672 0.1778 0.1778 0.0105 0.0084 0.2481 0.0602)
  ("Applicative [ ] that supports ‘vectorisation’, where pure = repeat and (~) = zapp," 0.1963 0.7988 0.1826 0.1931 0.1931 0.0105 0.0067 0.2682 0.0771)
  ("yielding" 0.1963 0.2535 0.1980 0.2085 0.2085 0.0105 nil 0.2999 0.0572)
  ("transpose :: [[a ]] → [[a ]]" 0.2381 0.4151 0.2196 0.2307 0.2307 0.0105 0.0037 0.4209 0.0662)
  ("transpose" 0.2381 0.3043 0.2356 0.2461 0.2461 0.0105 nil 0.1699 0.0662)
  ("[]" 0.3314 0.3435 0.2356 0.2461 0.2461 0.0105 nil nil 0.0121)
  ("^{=} J^{[ ]}K" 0.3775 0.4245 0.2356 0.2575 0.2575 0.0149 0.0046 0.5003 0.0130)
  ("transpose (xs : xss) = J (:) xs (transpose xss) K" 0.2381 0.5620 0.2510 0.2729 0.2615 0.0105 0.0047 0.2848 0.0662)
  ("In fact, repeat and zapp are not the return and ap of any Monad." 0.1963 0.6677 0.2730 0.2835 0.2835 0.0105 0.0056 0.2304 0.0153)
  ("3 Traversing data structures" 0.3766 0.6185 0.3132 0.3237 0.3237 0.0105 0.0064 0.1727 0.0096)
  ("Have you noticed that sequence and transpose now look rather alike? The details" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3364 0.3469 0.3469 0.0105 0.0066 0.2067 0.0363)
  ("that distinguish the two programs are inferred by the compiler from their types." 0.1963 0.7988 0.3518 0.3623 0.3623 0.0105 0.0068 0.2542 0.0307)
  ("Both are instances of the applicative distributor for lists:" 0.1963 0.6108 0.3671 0.3777 0.3777 0.0105 0.0056 0.2226 0.0360)
  ("dist :: Applicative f ⇒ [f a ] → f [a ]" 0.2381 0.4930 0.3887 0.3999 0.3999 0.0105 0.0046 0.4723 0.0251)
  ("dist [ ]" 0.2381 0.2799 0.4047 0.4153 0.4153 0.0105 0.0046 0.3337 0.0251)
  ("^{=} J^{[ ]}K" 0.3230 0.3700 0.4047 0.4267 0.4267 0.0149 0.0046 0.5000 0.0130)
  ("dist (v : vs) = J (:) v (dist vs) K" 0.2381 0.4530 0.4201 0.4421 0.4306 0.0105 0.0046 0.3403 0.0251)
  ("Distribution is often used together with ‘map’. For example, given the monadic" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4421 0.4526 0.4526 0.0105 0.0075 0.2738 0.0892)
  ("‘failure-propagation’ applicative functor for Maybe, we can map some failure-prone" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4575 0.4680 0.4680 0.0105 0.0052 0.2961 0.1484)
  ("operation (a function in a → Maybe b) across a list of inputs in such a way that" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4722 0.4834 0.4834 0.0105 0.0065 0.2641 0.0693)
  ("any individual failure causes failure overall." 0.1963 0.5137 0.4883 0.4988 0.4988 0.0105 0.0056 0.2493 0.0260)
  ("flakyMap :: (a → Maybe b) → [a ] → Maybe [b ]" 0.2381 0.5738 0.5098 0.5210 0.5210 0.0105 0.0046 0.4932 0.0643)
  ("flakyMap f ss = dist (fmap f ss)" 0.2381 0.4686 0.5258 0.5364 0.5364 0.0105 0.0046 0.3577 0.0643)
  ("As you can see, flakyMap traverses ss twice—once to apply f , and again to collect the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5478 0.5583 0.5583 0.0105 0.0038 0.3514 0.0191)
  ("results. More generally, it is preferable to define this applicative mapping operation" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5632 0.5737 0.5737 0.0105 0.0048 0.2963 0.0523)
  ("directly, with a single traversal:" 0.1963 0.4267 0.5786 0.5891 0.5891 0.0105 0.0056 0.2528 0.0586)
  ("traverse :: Applicative f ⇒ (a → f b) → [a ] → f [b ]" 0.2381 0.6050 0.6001 0.6113 0.6113 0.0105 0.0046 0.4798 0.0545)
  ("traverse f [ ]" 0.2381 0.3390 0.6162 0.6267 0.6267 0.0105 0.0047 0.6931 0.0545)
  ("^{=} J^{[ ]}K" 0.3661 0.4131 0.6162 0.6381 0.6381 0.0149 0.0046 0.5002 0.0130)
  ("traverse f (x : xs) = J (:) (f x ) (traverse f xs) K" 0.2381 0.5656 0.6316 0.6535 0.6421 0.0105 0.0047 0.2985 0.0545)
  ("This is just the way you would implement the ordinary fmap for lists, but with" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6535 0.6640 0.6640 0.0105 0.0073 0.2777 0.0326)
  ("the right-hand sides wrapped in J· · ·K, shifting them into the idiom. Just like fmap," 0.1963 0.7988 0.6683 0.6909 0.6794 0.0105 0.0052 0.3233 0.0232)
  ("traverse is a useful gadget to have for many data structures, hence we introduce the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6843 0.6948 0.6948 0.0105 0.0048 0.2516 0.0545)
  ("type class Traversable, capturing functorial data structures through which we can" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6997 0.7102 0.7102 0.0105 0.0063 0.2163 0.0321)
  ("thread an applicative computation:" 0.1963 0.4539 0.7151 0.7256 0.7256 0.0105 0.0056 0.2496 0.0475)
  ("class Traversable t where" 0.2381 0.4189 0.7373 0.7478 0.7478 0.0105 0.0047 0.2711 0.0354)
  ("traverse :: Applicative f ⇒ (a → f b) → t a" 0.2548 0.5655 0.7520 0.7632 0.7632 0.0105 0.0059 0.4294 0.0545)
  ("→ f (t b)" 0.5978 0.6661 0.7520 0.7632 0.7632 0.0105 0.0062 0.4737 0.0167)
  ("dist" 0.2548 0.2799 0.7680 0.7786 0.7786 0.0105 nil 0.2990 0.0251)
  (":: Applicative f ⇒" 0.3159 0.4435 0.7674 0.7786 0.7786 0.0105 0.0065 0.3121 0.0093)
  ("t (f a) → f (t a)" 0.5452 0.6672 0.7674 0.7786 0.7786 0.0105 0.0062 0.4055 0.0056)
  ("dist" 0.2548 0.2799 0.7834 0.7939 0.7939 0.0105 nil 0.2990 0.0251)
  ("= traverse id" 0.3140 0.4035 0.7834 0.7939 0.7939 0.0105 0.0047 0.3374 0.0130)
  ("Of course, we can recover an ordinary ‘map’ operator by taking f to be the identity—" 0.1963 0.7995 0.8054 0.8159 0.8159 0.0105 0.0037 0.3166 0.0181)
  ("the simple applicative functor in which all computations are pure:" 0.1963 0.6801 0.8208 0.8313 0.8313 0.0105 0.0056 0.2840 0.0232)
  ("newtype Id a = An{an :: a }" 0.2381 0.4389 0.8436 0.8548 0.8548 0.0105 0.0046 0.3720 0.0635))
  (6
  ("6" 0.1963 0.2046 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("Conor McBride and Ross Paterson" 0.3687 0.6263 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 0.0060 0.2641 0.0456)
  ("Haskell’s newtype declarations allow us to shunt the syntax of types around with-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1672 0.1778 0.1778 0.0105 0.0055 0.2560 0.0639)
  ("out changing the run-time notion of value or incurring any run-time cost. The" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1826 0.1931 0.1931 0.0105 0.0081 0.2335 0.0242)
  ("‘labelled field’ notation defines the projection an :: Id a → a at the same time as the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1973 0.2085 0.2085 0.0105 0.0049 0.3448 0.0609)
  ("constructor An :: a → Id a. The usual applicative functor has the usual application:" 0.1963 0.7988 0.2127 0.2239 0.2239 0.0105 0.0052 0.3078 0.0829)
  ("instance Applicative Id where" 0.2381 0.4501 0.2354 0.2459 0.2459 0.0105 0.0047 0.3076 0.0624)
  ("pure" 0.2548 0.2853 0.2508 0.2613 0.2613 0.0105 nil 0.1795 0.0304)
  ("= An" 0.3473 0.3848 0.2508 0.2613 0.2613 0.0105 0.0046 0.3737 0.0130)
  ("An f ~ An x = An (f x )" 0.2548 0.4256 0.2661 0.2785 0.2766 0.0105 0.0046 0.3997 0.0198)
  ("So, with the newtype signalling which Applicative functor to thread, we have" 0.1963 0.7592 0.2879 0.2984 0.2984 0.0105 0.0056 0.2775 0.0223)
  ("fmap f = an · traverse (An · f )" 0.3914 0.6037 0.3126 0.3238 0.3238 0.0105 0.0037 0.3836 0.0351)
  ("Meertens (1998) defined generic dist-like operators, families of functions of type" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3364 0.3469 0.3469 0.0105 0.0059 0.2709 0.0666)
  ("t (f a) → f (t a) for every regular functor t (that is, ‘ordinary’ uniform datatype" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3511 0.3623 0.3623 0.0105 0.0060 0.2761 0.0056)
  ("constructors with one parameter, constructed by recursive sums of products). His" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3672 0.3777 0.3777 0.0105 0.0061 0.2453 0.0895)
  ("conditions on f are satisfied by applicative functors, so the regular type constructors" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3826 0.3931 0.3931 0.0105 0.0041 0.2581 0.0745)
  ("can all be made instances of Traversable. The rule-of-thumb for traverse is ‘like fmap" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3979 0.4084 0.4084 0.0105 0.0047 0.3109 0.0251)
  ("but with J· · ·K on the right’. For example, here is the definition for trees:" 0.1963 0.7288 0.4127 0.4353 0.4238 0.0105 0.0056 0.2877 0.0251)
  ("data Tree a = Leaf | Node (Tree a) a (Tree a)" 0.2381 0.5654 0.4353 0.4465 0.4465 0.0105 0.0047 0.2861 0.0337)
  ("instance Traversable Tree where" 0.2381 0.4694 0.4547 0.4652 0.4652 0.0105 0.0047 0.2622 0.0624)
  ("traverse f Leaf" 0.2548 0.3635 0.4701 0.4806 0.4806 0.0105 0.0147 0.3193 0.0545)
  ("= J Leaf K" 0.4204 0.4861 0.4701 0.4920 0.4806 0.0149 0.0030 0.4870 0.0130)
  ("traverse f (Node l x r ) = J Node (traverse f l ) (f x ) (traverse f r ) K" 0.2548 0.7280 0.4855 0.5074 0.4960 0.0105 0.0047 0.3355 0.0545)
  ("This construction even works for non-regular types. However, not every Functor" 0.2130 0.7985 0.5072 0.5177 0.5177 0.0105 0.0058 0.2291 0.0326)
  ("is Traversable. For example, the functor (→) env cannot in general be Traversable." 0.1963 0.7988 0.5226 0.5331 0.5331 0.0105 0.0059 0.2702 0.0112)
  ("To see why, take env = Integer and try to distribute the Maybe functor!" 0.1963 0.7208 0.5380 0.5485 0.5485 0.0105 0.0056 0.2586 0.0191)
  ("Although Meertens did suggest that threading monads might always work, his" 0.2130 0.7988 0.5534 0.5639 0.5639 0.0105 0.0067 0.2667 0.0684)
  ("primary motivation was to generalise reduction or ‘crush’ operators, such as flat-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5688 0.5793 0.5793 0.0105 0.0065 0.2471 0.0582)
  ("tening trees and summing lists. We shall turn to these in the next section." 0.1963 0.7405 0.5841 0.5946 0.5946 0.0105 0.0056 0.2932 0.0456)
  ("4 Monoids are phantom Applicative functors" 0.3221 0.6730 0.6228 0.6333 0.6333 0.0105 0.0064 0.2936 0.0096)
  ("The data that one may sensibly accumulate have the Monoid structure:" 0.1963 0.7193 0.6459 0.6564 0.6564 0.0105 0.0056 0.2654 0.0288)
  ("class Monoid o where" 0.2381 0.3949 0.6678 0.6784 0.6784 0.0105 0.0047 0.3357 0.0354)
  ("∅ :: o" 0.2548 0.3062 0.6826 0.6937 0.6937 0.0112 0.0214 0.7730 0.0084)
  ("(⊕) :: o → o → o" 0.2548 0.3775 0.6979 0.7091 0.7091 0.0112 0.0046 0.5607 0.0260)
  ("such that ‘⊕’ is an associative operation with identity ∅. The functional program-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7203 0.7315 0.7315 0.0105 0.0059 0.2707 0.0322)
  ("ming world is full of monoids—numeric types (with respect to zero and plus, or one" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7364 0.7469 0.7469 0.0105 0.0047 0.3451 0.0363)
  ("and times), lists with respect to [ ] and ++, and many others—so generic technology" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7518 0.7623 0.7623 0.0105 0.0048 0.3364 0.0270)
  ("for working with them could well prove to be useful. Fortunately, every monoid" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7672 0.7777 0.7777 0.0105 0.0073 0.2541 0.0201)
  ("induces an applicative functor, albeit in a slightly peculiar way:" 0.1963 0.6624 0.7825 0.7930 0.7930 0.0105 0.0056 0.2550 0.0540)
  ("newtype Accy o a = Acc{acc :: o }" 0.2381 0.4861 0.8051 0.8163 0.8163 0.0105 0.0046 0.3371 0.0635)
  ("Accy o a is a phantom type (Leijen & Meijer, 1999)—its values have nothing to do" 0.1963 0.7988 0.8287 0.8393 0.8393 0.0105 0.0055 0.3057 0.0338)
  ("with a, but it does yield the applicative functor of accumulating computations:" 0.1963 0.7768 0.8441 0.8546 0.8546 0.0105 0.0056 0.2740 0.0326))))
